  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/1ffb0a83abf7e736c47035f4f337c36a6bb129fd0d64fe0ac3bfe8e46c194a11.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$",
